    {
      "post_id": 44107,
      "topic_id": 5404,
      "topic_title": "Fourier-Transform Holography",
      "forum_id": 30,
      "forum_name": "General Holography",
      "author": "Colin Kaminski",
      "author_id": null,
      "post_date": "Sat Feb 25, 2006 9:00 pm",
      "post_datetime": "2006-02-26T02:00:00+00:00",
      "post_subject": "Fourier-Transform Holography",
      "content_md": "A Fourier Hologram means that the complex amplitudes of the interference recorded are the Fourier Transforms (FT) of the complex amplitudes of the object and reference beams.\n\nThe traditional way to record one is to have an object and a reference beam coming from the same direction to wards the recording medium. In order for this to work the object needs to be a transparency. Note, there is a large 0 order emission during reconstruction and two images are formed during reconstruction corresponding to the +1 and -1 solutions of the FT.\n\nThis is not a method used for art holography although it makes neat lenses for glasses.\n\nAt least I think I am correct?\n\nOr were you looking for a more general answer that relates all holography to the FT.",
      "signature": "",
      "attachments": [],
      "quotes": [],
      "is_first_post": false,
      "url": "https://holographyforum.org/forum/viewtopic.php?p=44107#p44107"
    },
